# The nameplate less the demand, from the two filed totals, each end set against the opposite end.
# One row per layer that states both a demand and a nameplate, so both figures exist. The key is
# (filing, layer).
#
# Both figures come from one relation
#   layers/figures.sqlc sets each layer's demand beside its nameplate; this keeps the rows where
#   both are present.
#
# The ends are crossed
#   The low end of the remainder pairs the nameplate's low with the demand's high, because the
#   smallest remainder is the smallest supply meeting the largest demand. Get it backwards and every
#   remainder in the corpus comes out inside out, while every single number still looks plausible.
#
# A bound, not the remainder, on a composed layer
#   Crossing the ends assumes the two figures move independently, which on a composed layer they
#   need not. Where one conversion factor multiplied both the nameplate and the demand of a part,
#   pairing `n_low` with `d_high` takes that factor's range in twice, and the result is wider than
#   the remainder. On such a layer this relation is a bound, not the remainder;
#   `composition/fused_remainders.sqlc` works the remainder out by converting each part's own, and
#   `layers/remainder.sqlc` is the one that chooses between them.
#
# Every other layer agrees both ways
#   A single-valued factor, or none, leaves nothing to take in twice.
#   queries/observations/13-composed-remainder.sqlc prints the two figures side by side, and the
#   rows that agree are the control.
#
# The filed sign beside the worked-out one
#   The sign and the absorber are what the filing says, from layers/filed_remainders.sqlc. The join
#   is a LEFT JOIN because a layer may deny having a remainder and still state both figures; the
#   schema empties every part of a denied remainder, so such a layer carries the difference with
#   nothing filed beside it.
-- layers/figures.sqlc where both figures are present, beside the sign and absorber of
-- layers/filed_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT x.filing, x.layer,
       f.sign, f.sign_absent,
       f.absorber_taxonomy, f.absorber_value, f.absorber_absent,
       x.d_low, x.d_mode, x.d_high, x.d_unit AS unit,
       x.n_low, x.n_mode, x.n_high, x.n_unit AS amount_unit,
       x.n_low  - x.d_high AS r_low,   -- crossed: the least nameplate against the most demand
       x.n_mode - x.d_mode AS r_mode,
       x.n_high - x.d_low  AS r_high,
       CASE WHEN x.n_low  - x.d_high >= 0 THEN 'clearance'::pm.fit
            WHEN x.n_high - x.d_low  <= 0 THEN 'interference'::pm.fit
            ELSE 'transition'::pm.fit END AS derived_fit,
       greatest(x.d_high - x.n_low, 0) AS exposure,
       x.lumpy, x.quantum_mode, x.quantum_unit
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/figures.sqlc)
) x
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/filed_remainders.sqlc)
) f USING (filing, layer)
WHERE x.d_low IS NOT NULL
  AND x.n_low IS NOT NULL
